From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] windows.ui.xaml: theme colours and theme changes, x:Name keys, a library's own resources

WinUI 2's TabView (Windows Terminal's tab row) drew its add and close buttons as boxes in our default button face. Its templates use a style named with x:Name (TabViewButtonStyle) from its own generic.xaml, the icon font from that style, and brushes made from the system theme colours. A dictionary item named by x:Name and not keyed is now keyed by its name; a template's StaticResource and ThemeResource look in the dictionary named by its control's DefaultStyleResourceUri; <FontFamily> resources are read; FontFamily, FontSize, FontWeight, FontStyle and Foreground set on a control by a style reach the text inside it, as local ones did; and the SystemBase/Alt/Chrome/List colours are defined for the light and dark themes.
Windows Terminal then colours a tab by adding theme dictionaries to its TabViewItem and toggling the item's RequestedTheme: {ThemeResource} values are now those of the theme an element is drawn in (its RequestedTheme or its parents', else the application's) and are looked up again when a RequestedTheme above them changes -- local values, style setters and visual state setters, storyboards' values -- so the tab text is drawn in the colour Windows Terminal chose.
